               <p rend="justify">As primes become larger, the density of primes becomes smaller as 1/ln(n). Thus the density of true bits also falls off. The number of digits (binary or decimal) to represent the primes grows as ln(n). Thus, a sequence of primes represented as <hi rend="italic">primec</hi>  is always competitive in size with the corresponding sequence in ASCI text or binary, besides providing fast direct access by value:</p>
               <p rend="justify">bool isPrime(value).</p>
               <p rend="justify">The results for the sequence of the largest 64 bit primes (18446744073707000000 to 18446744073709551558) is:</p>
               <table rend="frame" xml:id="Table2">
                  <row>
                     <cell>Format</cell>
                     <cell>Size<lb/>(KB)</cell>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                     <cell>64 Bit Binary</cell>
                     <cell>445</cell>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                     <cell>Txt</cell>
                     <cell>1150</cell>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                     <cell>Zip Txt</cell>
                     <cell>114</cell>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                     <cell>PrimeC</cell>
                     <cell>125</cell>
                  </row>
               </table>
